#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151938 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152150 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "e49f7799-2724-5fe0-999f-bfa9ab5ce610" :question/math-academy-id "e-6244" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/4f/4faf932e63a57d2fe53fb3942cfe21c65d0d2a88692c665b2706bd68164855c9.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum,\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/26/26dc2a5af4c82657d9ec6fa331d4f5bc7575367bd4e3a00a5929b4ecaffe4477.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/6d/6d34737c12c35f6faec5caf37be500ba16c3f7cb18bf5ae660dc6d54182b66da.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=5 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,2)=6 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,3)=7 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(2,3)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =5+6+7+8=26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151939 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "1ac785c8-3d14-5968-94d3-6f4588f08d91" :question/math-academy-id "e-6245" :question/problem "Let $R = [1,5] \\times [2,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,3,5\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{2,4,6\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/51/5133b73c3707b87725baeee9a34a64898d33056dab9d7d32ac8cc24dbe772321.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ regions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{21}+4\\cdot {M}_{12}+4\\cdot {M}_{22} \\\\  & =4({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/5b/5b711e2c33e8966dfc9f4274a8f556f712d51a450bbcd0cadb68771fd050d304.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/ad/ad83cf465252b1f880757405ca3ff9ab5e1b9b1e50d712bd3b81305b7973af27.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=60 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(3,2)=52 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,4)=48 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(3,4)=40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4(60+52+48+40)=800.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151940 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/id #uuid "14e77998-b24b-56c1-8901-dc94d3ef98a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6246" :question/problem "![](images/33/33269c79413f4435d074d90cd727f2a53e7b9831300bfcaf0b5a7122df764080.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Find an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $2$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU = M_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = 8\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =8\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{21} \\\\  & =8{M}_{11}+4{M}_{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "548eea5d-4b28-5e16-b164-434012c34f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{12}\\cdot {M}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565812} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7eda2c-a027-5f95-97f6-e15eace33722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565826} {:answer/id #uuid "d79c99a8-657e-5f1b-9bf1-7b78ca1c5271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566027} {:answer/id #uuid "d9dffb21-59c0-5a1f-8919-0d8e4cb108a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}-{M}_{12}-{M}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566028} {:answer/id #uuid "e0699502-562e-5567-92d0-a0fdd69f0682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a7eda2c-a027-5f95-97f6-e15eace33722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565826} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b94767e-f6fc-5f2b-a935-cc47ec2a8213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f69d5203-16a9-5663-a85a-42dc609d0e31" :question/math-academy-id "q-122846" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac4970f243e32138114e2f8973205d39e75ed95445a0b410e969e494672d2b37.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=\\text{Area}({R}_{13})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =2\\cdot {M}_{11}+2\\cdot {M}_{12}+2\\cdot {M}_{13} \\\\  & =2({M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23926a61-03a3-5461-9946-25ae62757cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}+{M}_{41})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565737} {:answer/id #uuid "80a9b97e-e4af-5c15-9693-da5015493b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}+{M}_{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565881} {:answer/id #uuid "88571423-517e-51ee-b66f-67c0f785fd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+2{M}_{21}+2{M}_{31}+{M}_{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565893} {:answer/id #uuid "97812a4d-76b5-5d6e-b1ae-dc5df4c4141b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{21}\\cdot {M}_{31}\\cdot {M}_{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565920} {:answer/id #uuid "dca45719-25c3-5ae1-83e9-c4706be64e6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}+2{M}_{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23926a61-03a3-5461-9946-25ae62757cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}+{M}_{41})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565737} :answer-field/id #uuid "57f9cf12-8dfb-53b1-b292-86ced016edb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b6196d0-946b-5675-b1cf-30fba169b2db" :question/math-academy-id "q-123056" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6dcc08ca41977f189d4e873be6c060b3d8f446fb3ba2e4b0ee2667403ebb07fc.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31})+{M}_{41}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{41}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=\\text{Area}({R}_{31})=\\text{Area}({R}_{41})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =2\\cdot {M}_{11}+2\\cdot {M}_{21}+2\\cdot {M}_{31}+2\\cdot {M}_{41} \\\\  & =2({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}+{M}_{41}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ec49a8-4c66-57e0-837c-295202d2c300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+6{M}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542637} {:answer/id #uuid "2f3098d3-875b-59ab-8cac-dfd53d3cd743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cdot {M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{21}\\cdot {M}_{12}\\cdot {M}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542764} {:answer/id #uuid "370d35e1-74cd-5762-b7ee-1e0055383a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+6{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542784} {:answer/id #uuid "6f04ca05-8b47-57ce-86ea-b16c43610fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542934} {:answer/id #uuid "b766cfd8-6c6f-5a73-8c80-f2d0880c659a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b766cfd8-6c6f-5a73-8c80-f2d0880c659a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543118} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fb99eb4-e0b4-5357-8ce8-08584c6260b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3f890f2-0602-53b4-b697-bf1bfc15cf9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123057" :question/problem "![](images/16/16e592cd81e6539e26f10b6bd9352b047576df24b74b4df693f5f2f69799b228.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=\\text{Area}({R}_{22})=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =6\\cdot {M}_{11}+6\\cdot {M}_{21}+6\\cdot {M}_{12}+6\\cdot {M}_{22} \\\\  & =6({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013eb8ba-2e75-572b-b420-54178b0eb197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4({M}_{11}+{M}_{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565677} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3d9435-4690-5e73-b1cf-047a2751112a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{12}-{M}_{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565880} {:answer/id #uuid "c5b9a51f-45a8-5f29-bd32-e075b761e09e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+4{M}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565995} {:answer/id #uuid "cde0b272-300e-514e-be66-91ac196872ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+{M}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566013} {:answer/id #uuid "ea08442c-fd3b-5555-a6e4-c06d13e975c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{M}_{11}+{M}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "013eb8ba-2e75-572b-b420-54178b0eb197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4({M}_{11}+{M}_{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565677} :answer-field/id #uuid "fea3a60f-daf8-541c-b5ca-7c7fb29e64dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7b6e192-3a06-52fc-8d5f-7a6810288b2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-123058" :question/problem "![](images/dd/dd73cb7785de514503cba6a04931a88084172b58826efdf22ea847f256c375fb.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $2$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =4\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{12} \\\\  & =4({M}_{11}+{M}_{12}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01d4ba0e-ab3b-5599-869a-41c3240198d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565680} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2e9ef1-c4da-5fc7-be4b-026f8b0e945d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565795} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd2e9bb-e861-580e-9f16-5b718d107429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565830} {:answer/id #uuid "90b1f25d-f8da-5944-b0cb-c5c49e1e39ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{M}_{11}+3{M}_{21}-{M}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565909} {:answer/id #uuid "c67b7698-5934-5992-8207-9d8f7538d1b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15{M}_{11}+5{M}_{21}+3{M}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01d4ba0e-ab3b-5599-869a-41c3240198d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565680} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aa326de-d714-5b39-9140-e8acb0109c83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1acef96d-8256-5e1a-85ce-bb35520d6331" :question/math-academy-id "q-123125" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4ab905de5bed2182757fa11a1945bbffec175b314918b3581d2ae062d64cdcc.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=\\text{Area}({R}_{31})=15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =15\\cdot {M}_{11}+15\\cdot {M}_{21}+15\\cdot {M}_{31} \\\\  & =15({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152150 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aae757b2-ad20-5f73-bc19-a111718bcefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148920} {:answer/id #uuid "b8976c57-12da-5ea3-be35-6374777492d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "448" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148921} {:answer/id #uuid "bf66b110-4f82-502b-9721-4f858b3f85b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148922} {:answer/id #uuid "e24febc7-0459-5c41-a48b-36604cec9050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148923} {:answer/id #uuid "fcab21c2-a85a-5473-a3de-68f4de5e1079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8976c57-12da-5ea3-be35-6374777492d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "448" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148921} :answer-field/id #uuid "8be62ba8-d31a-5784-9045-50df8ca84da1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c4585e46-b2c8-5baf-a8e2-9d0a8e6d3871" :question/math-academy-id "q-123133" :question/problem "Let $R=[2,10]\\times [3,7]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{2,6,10\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{3,5,7\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/09/0938d12d93c4f962a4ebd8daa7efe4543fc30c419be7d89f165e21bcadce84bb.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=8$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =8\\cdot {M}_{11}+8\\cdot {M}_{21}+8\\cdot {M}_{12}+8\\cdot {M}_{22} \\\\  & =8({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/af/af324138b21609cef59f49df0920ecc12681b8cb7667a843ff2d3fff3c27ea13.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/14/14b50472bce6d935803ca4f74bb5b1761903c0b0b015ce72e7e1e5e08f35868b.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(6,5)=11 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(10,5)=15 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(6,7)=13 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(10,7)=17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =8(11+15+13+17) \\\\  & =448.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2163f261-b0e3-5afe-af6d-ae5ea46969f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542730} {:answer/id #uuid "398097ad-1342-5db5-93d2-00dc4659d2ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542792} {:answer/id #uuid "3989a74d-2dc1-572b-bd50-bd4dc84f2a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542793} {:answer/id #uuid "50307643-fa90-566b-bcf8-3b0e643e8d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542858} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a69bed-2b30-5998-a7d0-481c3cc1de3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3989a74d-2dc1-572b-bd50-bd4dc84f2a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542793} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd3b050-eb07-5223-9aa5-20d0a7f35114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d30a7455-cf28-573b-a7f5-088c618e903c" :question/math-academy-id "q-123231" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,4]\\times [2,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=16-xy.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{2,4\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/de/deff4f8b668e3c7327d92132f579c16d1f99dd5b52603b2f6afe6b7c6c5b7bb6.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =2\\cdot {M}_{11}+2\\cdot {M}_{21}+2\\cdot {M}_{31} \\\\  & =2({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=16-xy$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=16-xy$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/21/21cd2d1c002e5b66100396f9e4d5708b8ffd7fd2b9b6cc7b7078cd5507011a0d.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/9e/9ebca45ee50cf0fe5f91e21427524600b26a512f10340c18c995c16f9cfe372d.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=14 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,2)=12 \\\\ {M}_{31} & =f(3,2)=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =2(14+12+10) \\\\  & =72.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39917bfb-b047-56d8-b47c-c5ec66b1e25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565772} {:answer/id #uuid "443a2ad6-b0fb-5a5a-a98a-17ea5dd555e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565787} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4eb9aa-414e-5ce6-bdbf-2a9bea7a24d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565874} {:answer/id #uuid "b22e9aaf-cb1e-5405-8f98-df2a5454337d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565961} {:answer/id #uuid "f946a5c4-dc1b-5486-a418-99decd3f1256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b22e9aaf-cb1e-5405-8f98-df2a5454337d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565961} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fc70692-4870-59d7-8ebd-70a7bca018cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50cce965-116a-5091-8060-d4b135e273bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-123232" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,5]\\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=10-x-{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,1\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/5b/5bd8200cb0f0aa8ca38548ae731acb54d7118e3ca735ebd5e4aebe5b6a7aa1b5.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31})+{M}_{41}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{41}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}+{M}_{41}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-x-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-x-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/cc/cc616280fc457f14441e30a9e551eacf73bd56de02e68ced200302cbb1db68ac.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/e2/e2e1ab24e5d73cfc6414d7e01cd2d2d923f0f73bca137e65c9968b9142360389.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,0)=9 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,0)=8 \\\\ {M}_{31} & =f(3,0)=7 \\\\ {M}_{41} & =f(4,0)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =9+8+7+6 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1554cd6c-bc48-5357-bfb6-fd992f56f2c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565713} {:answer/id #uuid "15957473-8159-5ecd-bb20-e1513dd4ecde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,350" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565715} {:answer/id #uuid "9a87d8cc-135e-5a20-8918-9957d54d7ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565925} {:answer/id #uuid "c2224caf-3cbf-5e50-a2f8-443aa9d623a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "170" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565988} {:answer/id #uuid "d3edb6e3-1c8e-5e1f-8baa-842b14f66295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1554cd6c-bc48-5357-bfb6-fd992f56f2c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565713} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cc8f80d-aaaf-5d96-872e-79594ef03ec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0e69171-a8d5-534f-acb8-6c56c972aa07" :question/math-academy-id "q-123243" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,5]\\times [1,5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=100-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,5\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,3,5\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/77/77fdf238fb2b4046da6ddc69024378a56e4de5f46de301d5fac094cbc290b23b.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=10$ for both subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =10\\cdot {M}_{11}+10\\cdot {M}_{12} \\\\  & =10({M}_{11}+{M}_{12}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=100-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=100-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/62/62871cd2fcc4993e0775fb7bdf0609d7849a1aa2ea5a314bc727967df590d6e7.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/9b/9b814374a5b132560656d6fe2dc2350cb693847733a40c0d21b9a13be30025e1.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(0,1)=97 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(0,3)=73\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =10(97+73) \\\\  & =1\\,700.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152150 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47143193-8197-59a4-a2da-4be68703689e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565790} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff307b3-0cf9-5782-bf27-1ba7036e9b66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565804} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e04a2c-361f-56eb-bed1-233836e23e41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565969} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ec27bf-c690-5989-8d49-e61940286e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565973} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e8b813-b4ae-570d-81ba-bcfa60a66d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4e04a2c-361f-56eb-bed1-233836e23e41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565969} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8792aef-971c-524f-813b-450f77f5e710" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f88ad5cd-8533-5739-b1c9-9a040c2e3b2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75045" :question/problem "Let $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{-1,0,1\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{-1,0,1\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/0c/0c68391a7a23867f21ee851aa86d389ff4dc186c3aaedde0ccb1fe15b5b4ee5b.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+2$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+2$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/14/144142b823e7db82eb9b65e6ddc835d75a7fd8d218eab17f9db9019c4b8ab109.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/a2/a2c948117d63fdd60d16ac29b3900238f7c246201bd45df3e3ee940aac0d4ee6.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(0,0)=2 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(1,0)=3 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(0,1)=3 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(1,1)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =2+3+3+4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152150 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b76a90e1-0093-5557-bfa6-336787d0228e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150780} {:answer/id #uuid "d60e774d-104e-5f8d-9cc4-b20acaef5d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150781} {:answer/id #uuid "e53e2ada-6d52-5dda-b9e3-37cce39a3c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150782} {:answer/id #uuid "510f1120-7e20-58a5-be36-a89b41dbdb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150783} {:answer/id #uuid "72b0f521-a660-51fd-ae7d-ee19d27ee7e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72b0f521-a660-51fd-ae7d-ee19d27ee7e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150784} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ba5c66a-45bc-5d4b-a390-8fdb670f2bcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "122fa252-520a-5995-91e9-6f1ba7a38a89" :question/math-academy-id "q-75047" :question/problem "Let $R=[2,4]\\times [2,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{2,3,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{2,3,4\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/e8/e88b9d4b756f76a8b7c75287f0c4e3ff07f7b458dcdcea703769fc6da56f2a12.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc9b8c4634691d17614c39e542488fb17d2918737be0dad5d262cd9a1fd827a.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/be/beb238668c9c1ebb66f8c7ec3cd8a40b267b47d7ba1cbd2e351c65b3c17d7a84.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(3,3)=18 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(4,3)=25 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(3,4)=25 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(4,4)=32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =18+25+25+32 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152150 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b4d37c-af3f-5a0b-b1a5-aa4f21cf5ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542706} {:answer/id #uuid "2a88b7ea-918e-5f5d-b524-932d57163e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542752} {:answer/id #uuid "75856a7f-2f29-5d07-acad-a77f3e052314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542949} {:answer/id #uuid "907c182d-7aff-57bd-84fa-04408cb79bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543017} {:answer/id #uuid "a852062d-d706-5f9f-9981-e973136b8709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19b4d37c-af3f-5a0b-b1a5-aa4f21cf5ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542706} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e998d72-50f3-55e6-8d59-b1ab24f7504c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4bd06db2-69af-51a2-9fd5-21474b678038" :question/math-academy-id "q-75176" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,4]\\times [2,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+2y-3.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{2,3,4\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/89/89c82b8d1f3eaf79c7df0a67f377a240a949dda328cd2f448370077d2871c244.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}) \\\\  & \\,+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})+{M}_{32}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{32}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $6$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}+{M}_{32}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+2y-3$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+2y-3$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/78/7888eaa17b26267cc8bed166500ba504fe5a893b30eb90876f2074520e28e8ba.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/ea/eadef808ebc8ff5503de8cefbbbf3a1d64bf3149f0c0c80166c891550ddee91d.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(2,3)=5 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(3,3)=6 \\\\ {M}_{31} & =f(4,3)=7 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(2,4)=7 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(3,4)=8 \\\\ {M}_{32} & =f(4,4)=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =5+6+7+7+8+9 \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152150 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ce39bb5-a799-5b6e-9a15-dceec85eb578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565877} {:answer/id #uuid "84adaa8f-d6bb-51e6-a982-60965661513b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565886} {:answer/id #uuid "8761081f-ad94-57ba-80a7-1efbf007857c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565890} {:answer/id #uuid "f04285fe-0309-56d8-804f-d0e703b8541f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566074} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f66292-0731-5d0f-b07b-26cc33e74a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f04285fe-0309-56d8-804f-d0e703b8541f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566074} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fb061b0-ea4e-5a2f-871b-5b70b09fe31c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4fe7c63-ddd1-59b8-9a3b-8f1a0e46d1f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-75184" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+5.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/6f/6fc1abc4b93b59eda024e126f8503b23f067bd9ea086a0256fcd3e67dfd7200a.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+5$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+5$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/7d/7dbec95d53ae55a09ab0a941c69f506f11be6e645a03aca3095bede1b3e966f8.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/5e/5edcabe01ac87d115a69a8b842c537187b1f1d2a003664e5fb68f7e70db3ae3d.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(2,2)=17 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(3,2)=27 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(2,3)=22 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(3,3)=32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =17+27+22+32 \\\\  & =98.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a00637db-de9b-56ed-a098-59411cde942a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150822} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a55e44-b375-54dd-a983-e6ed8acae1ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150823} {:answer/id #uuid "b92a08bf-50c4-51af-847d-a2e83b44b73f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150824} {:answer/id #uuid "6922a0cc-0640-5694-b856-0f882a575714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150825} {:answer/id #uuid "7eea493d-44d4-59ee-941b-3b758b85f0e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6922a0cc-0640-5694-b856-0f882a575714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150825} :answer-field/id #uuid "14edd6b1-b94a-57fc-9aaf-d788b0b6d33e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "27fbd536-fbac-5629-a345-3477109a83e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-75229" :question/problem "Let $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=6-2x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{-2,0,2\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{-2,0,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/c8/c8b7ffacce26030414c0e319624f5c171d28599f0cf9375e40ddaf252d6b7380.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{21}+4\\cdot {M}_{12}+4\\cdot {M}_{22} \\\\  & =4({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=6-2x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=6-2x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/77/770593c18eaaebff55c73151b0e98628bd4ece00b75380b29fde8d1721f1cf16.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc8062cb5c436a0135abd2340fcaa39560658c4a23f13f0cb70893789a81836.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(-2,-2)=12 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(0,-2)=8 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(-2,0)=10 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(0,0)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4(12+8+10+6) \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "342a7334-6ebf-59b7-8331-37af5e6b4d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565759} {:answer/id #uuid "50aeb266-ab0d-5212-9160-89d504220b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565807} {:answer/id #uuid "7b106611-ae0e-5111-91d1-a39c2610f56d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565872} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1a5a83-6b20-57f5-b4fb-8472a5b67928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565903} {:answer/id #uuid "de92d4f3-fc6c-5524-b29c-1a39a0e93889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50aeb266-ab0d-5212-9160-89d504220b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565807} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d634307-c7b5-59ff-9b60-846917abd3ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "003282d8-d3cb-54da-a9af-399e3a75da3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-75234" :question/problem "Let $R=[-3,-1]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=10-4x-2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{-3,-2,-1\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/10/10eca85fc2ab60ea0b1ee229b0a0e20110f88c4724088c0f0630d22150a8a08d.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}+{M}_{41}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-4x-2y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-4x-2y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/ea/eaa3ed265eac5a53eedc07f07e2ff386505a6ea5cc4934653d587e84238b540d.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/c3/c332690fba1843ee09bd1466f1c978c41aff8438bf9b2a23a847ca58e2f9c228.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(-3,1)=20 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(-2,1)=16 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(-3,2)=18 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(-2,2)=14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =20+16+18+14 \\\\  & =68.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
